    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=919515": {
      "title": "Events Coordinator",
      "description": "Events Coordinator Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 24/10/2026 Part-time, 0.8 FTE, fixed term to June 2028 l Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Anticipated start date: January 2027 Reference: 1688004 About the Role: The Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES ) is seeking an experienced and highly organised Events Coordinator to plan and deliver events that support our priorities, stakeholder engagement, and sector impact. Working under the broad direction of the Communications and Stakeholder Engagement Manager, you will coordinate events from initial planning through to post-event evaluation. This includes managing logistics, registrations, speakers, venues, suppliers, budgets, and on-the-day delivery. You will also prepare event-related communications and contribute to broader communications activities as part of the ACSES Communications team. Your responsibilities will include: Plan and coordinate in-person, online, and hybrid events from concept to completion. Manage event logistics, including venues, catering, audio-visual requirements, accessibility, suppliers, and event materials. Prepare event documentation, including schedules, run sheets, briefings, attendee lists, and risk documentation. Manage registrations and communications with attendees, speakers, facilitators, and other contributors. Monitor budgets, obtain quotes, process invoices, and maintain accurate records. Oversee on-the-day delivery, including registrations, speaker support, supplier coordination, program timing, and troubleshooting. Liaise professionally with internal and external stakeholders, including sector partners, government representatives, and suppliers. Prepare event-related communications and promotional content across digital and print channels. Evaluate event delivery and recommend improvements for future activities. Who we are looking for: You will be an adaptable and detail-oriented events professional with experience coordinating events through their full lifecycle. You will be comfortable managing competing priorities, working with diverse stakeholders, and responding calmly when circumstances change. You will also have strong written communication skills and an understanding of the importance of accessible, inclusive, and professionally delivered events. Selection Criteria To be considered for this position, your application should clearly address the following selection criteria. Please use specific examples to demonstrate your experience. Essential Demonstrated experience coordinating and delivering in-person, online, and hybrid events from planning through to completion. Demonstrated ability to anticipate and resolve problems during event planning and delivery, exercise sound judgement, and respond calmly when circumstances change. Experience preparing clear event-related communications for channels such as registration platforms, newsletters, email, and social media. Strong organisational and project coordination skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, maintain accurate event documentation, and meet firm deadlines. High-level interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated experience coordinating speakers and liaising professionally with senior stakeholders, event participants, colleagues, and external suppliers. Desirable : Knowledge or experience of the Australian tertiary education sector. Broader experience contributing to communications and stakeholder engagement activities. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Employment type: 0.8 FTE, with consideration given to full-time (1.0 FTE) for the right candidate. Anticipated start date: January 2027. Your new Team: Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES ) is a national research and policy centre funded by the Australian Government Department of Education and based at Curtin University. Our core mission is to bridge the gap between research, policy, and practice in higher education to promote equity. We conduct a wide range of activities, including research projects, grants programs, policy evaluation, fellowships, data analysis, publications, events, and more. We are committed to building a robust evidence base that informs evidence-based policy and institutional best practices, making a real impact on higher education accessibility. For more information on ACSES visit www.acses.edu.au, or for more information about Curtin, please visit www.curtin.edu.au. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term G06 level role attracts a remuneration of $99,213 - $105,251 pro rata for (0.8) FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 8th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=918993": {
      "title": "Project Administrator",
      "description": "Project Administrator Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 22/10/2026 Fixed term, part-time (0.6 FTE) contract until 30 June 2028 Work alongside engineers, astronomers, data scientists and technical experts On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Reference Number: 1697334 About the Team: Radio Astronomy applies ‘STEM’ based disciplines to capture and analyse ultra-faint signals from the universe. In doing this, we push the boundaries of technology and techniques in electromagnetics, signal processing, software development, and radio frequency communications. The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) and The International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) deliver a dedicated Translation and Impact program (T&I) to support the translation of our core astronomy and engineering expertise, skills, knowledge, and technology for impact in industries like space and defence - to name a few. The T&I team enables ICRAR’s technical staff to deliver impact by providing broad support to the technical experts who deliver on collaborative projects. About the Role: As Project Administrator, you will provide high level administrative and operational project support to the T&I program and individual industry-research projects, assisting with the development, coordination, and implementation of project deliverables under the guidance of the project lead. To excel in providing operational project support, you will draw on previous exposure to a technical or scientific discipline to understand CIRA’s engineering capabilities. As part of the multi-disciplinary T&I team, you will engage with diverse stakeholders – from academic researchers and students (engineers, astronomers, data scientists, software developers) – to industry and government professionals (including defence, space, computing, resources, climate, weather, environment).To succeed in this, you will need to be responsive and service oriented, with strong written and verbal communication skills and excellent attention to detail. Administratively, you will support the T&I program and industry research projects with excellent organisational skills, attention to detail in document management, planning and tracking activities for reporting purposes, and ensuring quality and accuracy in all communications. You will be responsible for creating materials which are visually compelling, and tailored to relevant audiences. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinating and preparing regular and ad hoc project reports, e.g. status reports and end of month reporting requirements, such as project forecasting expenditure information and accruals. Maintaining project registers, databases, artefacts and documentation. Liaising with relevant external stakeholders and University service functions (e.g. People and Culture, Financial Services) to ensure relevant actions are completed. Working under the guidance of the project lead to support technical project delivery teams with: field trip preparation and logistics, including assistance in preparing risk assessments and safety protocols; procurement of technical services and supplies; and preparation of documents required for the export of certain technical goods and services, including Defence Export Controls and other customs requirements, ensuring compliance with Curtin University policies and procedures. Liaising with project teams to facilitate data capture and verifying milestones/activity. Facilitating information flow, administering communications and correspondence and distributing visually compelling and precise information in a professional and timely manner. Who We Are Looking For (Selection Criteria): We are looking for someone personable and driven who may see this as an opportunity to advance their project or program management aspirations. In this role, your previous exposure to a physical science, computing, or engineering discipline will complement your business administration and operations support. You will be comfortable working alongside technical subject matter experts to understand and communicate effectively our industry-relevant capabilities. Additionally, you will demonstrate skills and experience in the following Essential Criteria : Demonstrated ability to gather, synthesise, and present written and visually compelling material for a variety of audiences. Completion of a relevant degree; or an equivalent combination of relevant work experience and/or education/training. Demonstrated excellent organisational skills with the ability to organise time and work activities according to priorities and to meet deadlines. Demonstrated interpersonal and oral communication skills with the ability to consult and liaise effectively with a diverse range of people and with cross cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated effective written communication skills with the ability to construct clear and concise correspondence and documentation. Proven ability to work well in a team, including respecting difference, leading, supporting others, accepting direction, and working to group goals. Demonstrated research and problem-solving skills. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This is a part-time role – up to 0.6FTE; we will consider requests for flexible working hours including requests to work over 3 to 5 days a week. Your new Team – CIRA: The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) is a multi-disciplinary research institute engaged in a range of projects involving or motivated by radio astronomy. CIRA’s activities draw together academic research and professional staff from a variety of science, engineering, and computing disciplines to tackle unique and demanding challenges in astronomy and non-astronomy domains. Committed to research translation and impact (T&I), CIRA is committed to engaging with partners to share our skills, knowledge, and expertise for mutual benefit and impact beyond astronomy. CIRA is the lead organisation and operator of the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA), a key participant in the international SKA Project. CIRA is also the Curtin University node of the International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) which was established in 2009, and one of the largest and most enduring academic research initiatives in Western Australia. CIRA celebrates diversity and is an equal opportunity employer. We encourage applications from all cultures, races, nationalities, genders, identities, and abilities aligning with the selection criteria, and are committed to preserving our principles of equitable and inclusive work and study environments. To learn more about CIRA, please visit the below websites: https://research.curtin.edu.au/cira/ www.icrar.org About Curtin University We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This fixed term, part-time G05 level role attracts a remuneration of $85,781 - $93,836 (pro rata at 0.6 FTE) + 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest, experience and addressing the section criteria for the role (no more than two pages) Applications close: 8pm, Monday, 9 November 2026 If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=918081": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Teaching and Research",
      "description": "Lecturer - Teaching and Research Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 21/10/2026 Ongoing role, Full-time, Bentley Campus Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation & collaboration Reference: 1696073 About the Role: The School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry (MCASI) at Curtin University is seeking a Lecturer (Academic Level B) in Libraries, Archives, Records and Information Science (LARIS). Based at our Bentley campus in Perth, this Teaching and Research position spans two closely connected discipline areas: Library and Information Science and Recordkeeping and Archives. You will contribute to postgraduate teaching, unit coordination and research, helping prepare students for professional practice in libraries, archives, records management and information services. Working with colleagues and industry partners, you will connect teaching and research with contemporary professional practice and support opportunities for students to gain workplace experience. In this role, you will: Deliver high-quality postgraduate teaching and undertake unit coordination across Library and Information Science, and Recordkeeping and Archives, including assessment, curriculum development and ongoing reflective improvement of teaching practice. Develop a coherent program of high-quality research within LARIS, publish in refereed journals and other discipline-appropriate outlets, contribute to competitive research funding applications, disseminate findings through conferences and other scholarly forums, and contribute to the development of teaching and research programs. Build collaborative relationships with industry, national and state organisations, and research end-users. Support work-integrated learning opportunities that connect students with professional practice. Contribute to academic service, professional and community engagement, and a collegial and supportive working environment, consistent with University policies and professional standards. Contribute to the supervision of PhD and master’s research students. Selection Criteria: Your application should demonstrate how you meet the following essential selection criteria: A doctoral qualification and relevant expertise in Library and Information Science, Archives and/or Recordkeeping. Demonstrated high-level teaching skills and a reflective approach to delivering and continuously improving teaching. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline, together with a record of contribution to the scholarship of teaching. An established record of research outputs in high-quality refereed journals or other discipline-appropriate outlets. A history of successful competitive grant applications. Demonstrated ability to supervise higher degree research students. Demonstrated experience in, or capacity to undertake, unit coordination. Demonstrated high-level communication and interpersonal skills, a collaborative approach to academic work, a commitment to fostering a collegial and supportive working environment, and the ability to interact with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day responsibilities of the position. Desirable: Professional experience as a librarian, archivist, information scientist or records manager is desirable. About us The Libraries, Archives, Records and Information Science (LARIS) area sits within the iSchool discipline of MCASI. Research across the iSchool encompasses open access and open science, social media and communications, research evaluation and more. Our staff have strong ties with other areas including Internet Studies, Journalism and the Curtin Open Knowledge Initiative (COKI), creating opportunities for collaboration across disciplines. MCASI is a member of iSchools Inc. and maintains links with international and national professional associations in the LARIS fields. Our courses connect critical information practice with global information issues and the Australian information sector. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $121,857 - $144,352 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) 2 - 4 Pages max If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Tuesday 10th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=918109": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Teaching & Research",
      "description": "Lecturer - Teaching & Research Faculty of Business & Law Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 21/10/2026 Fixed-term, Part time 0.5 FTE contract until 31st December 2027 Based on Bentley Campus Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference: 1687525 About the Role: This role offers an early career academic the opportunity to advance their teaching, research and engagement in human resource management, organisational behaviour, management and related disciplines. You will deliver undergraduate teaching and, depending on your experience and expertise, postgraduate teaching, while contributing to innovative curriculum and learning content. You will have opportunities to develop your Higher Degree by Research (HDR) supervision and leadership experience, supported by mentoring and professional development appropriate to your career stage. Mentoring will also support academics with limited HDR supervision experience and help you build industry partnerships that enrich your teaching and research. Your responsibilities will include: Design and deliver high-quality, innovative undergraduate teaching in human resource management, employment relations and related disciplines, and postgraduate teaching where appropriate to your experience and expertise. Develop curriculum, learning materials and assessments that reflect contemporary and emerging labour market trends and align with program learning objectives. Provide academic guidance and support to students, including advice on postgraduate study pathways. Conduct high-quality research and publish in leading outlets, including A*/A-ranked management journals. Identify external funding opportunities, develop and submit competitive applications, and secure funding to support research activities. Build relationships with industry partners, policymakers and other stakeholders to identify research needs and develop demand-driven research opportunities. Participate in professional development and contribute to internal and external service and engagement activities. Contribute to a positive culture across the School, Faculty and University, reflecting Curtin’s values of Respect, Integrity, Impact, Courage and Excellence. Undertake other duties as directed. Who We Are Looking For/Selection Criteria: A doctoral qualification in a relevant academic discipline (Human Resource Management, Organisational Behaviour, Management). Demonstrated ability to teach at undergraduate and/or postgraduate levels, and to develop innovative and high-quality course materials in accordance with their career stage. A track record of achievement in and commitment to excellence in delivering learning experiences across multiple teaching modes and platforms in accordance with their career stage. A record of high-quality research commensurate with their career stage as evidenced by publications in relevant disciplines (Human Resource Management, organisational behaviour, management), and a clear plan to build a high-quality publications pipeline. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role This is a part-time role (0.5 FTE), with working days to be discussed at interview. Applicants are welcome to indicate their preferred working days in their cover letter. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $121,857 - $144,352 (ALB) pro rata to (FTE) FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) – 2 – 3 Pages max If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Tuesday 3rd of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=918320": {
      "title": "Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous (s50d)",
      "description": "Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous (s50d) Office of the Vice Chancellor Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 21/10/2026 Inaugural senior leadership role within Curtin's DVC Indigenous portfolio Full-time, five-year leadership appointment | based at Curtin's Bentley Campus Bring people together to support meaningful change Job Reference: 1665640 In line with Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Plan, Curtin University is committed to optimising employment outcomes for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples. Curtin University considers that being an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person is a genuine occupational requirement for this position under section 50(d) of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA). Therefore, only applications from Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people will be considered. Curtin University is seeking an outstanding academic leader to join our newly established Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous Portfolio as Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous. Working closely with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous, you will listen to and learn from the people, knowledge and work already present across Curtin, bring colleagues together around a shared vision, and translate the University’s Indigenous strategic priorities into practical academic frameworks, initiatives and outcomes. We are looking for someone with the academic standing and research credibility to lead and influence at the highest levels of the University, but just as importantly, someone who is deeply relational. You will be a thoughtful listener, a trusted partner and a natural connector who understands that meaningful institutional change is built through strong relationships, shared ownership and a genuine commitment to people. While this is a new role, you will be supported by a warm and closely connected team that is genuinely committed to working together and making a difference. The role does not currently have direct reports; however, you will help shape the portfolio’s future capability and, as the work grows, play an important role in building and leading a team. This position will be central to Curtin’s forthcoming Indigenous Strategy and the achievement of one of the University’s key strategic priorities. It is a genuine opportunity to create strong foundations, influence how the University works and contribute to meaningful outcomes for current and future generations of researchers and students. The Opportunity At Curtin, we are deeply committed to placing the voices, knowledges and perspectives of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples at the forefront of our decision-making and creating a University in which everyone can thrive. The Associate Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous will play an important role in bringing this commitment to life across Curtin’s academic activities. Operating under the strategic direction of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous, you will provide delegated University-wide leadership across Indigenous learning and teaching, curriculum transformation, research and Higher Degree by Research pathways, and broader academic capability development. This is both a significant academic leadership position and a highly collaborative role. You will work in partnership with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic, Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research, Faculty Deans Indigenous, the Centre for Aboriginal Studies, faculties, research institutes and professional portfolios to develop coordinated approaches that deliver meaningful and measurable outcomes. As an inaugural appointment within the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous portfolio, you will have a unique opportunity to help shape the role, establish priorities and contribute to the development of the University's approach to Indigenous academic leadership. Drawing on strengths already present across Curtin, you will identify opportunities, foster collaboration and help create the structures and momentum needed to support lasting change. In this role, you will Provide visible and trusted academic leadership across Indigenous learning, teaching, research and Higher Degree by Research pathways. Help translate Curtin’s Indigenous Strategy into coordinated initiatives that deliver meaningful and measurable outcomes. Champion the embedding of Indigenous knowledges, perspectives and pedagogies across curriculum, research and academic practice. Strengthen Indigenous-led and Indigenous-engaged research, researcher capability, collaboration and impact. Build respectful and reciprocal relationships across Curtin and with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and sector partners. Lead and support a connected, high-performing team while establishing the governance and shared accountability needed to sustain progress. Represent Curtin and advocate for Indigenous scholarship, excellence and success across the University and broader higher education sector. About you We are looking for an accomplished academic leader with the standing, experience and credibility to influence across a complex university environment. You will bring a strong research background and a deep understanding of Indigenous higher education, research and policy in Australia. You will be a builder who is energised by the opportunity to shape an emerging portfolio. You will value and respect the important work already underway across Curtin while being prepared to ask thoughtful questions, connect areas of expertise and develop new approaches that strengthen the University’s collective impact. You will bring: A PhD or equivalent record of scholarly, professional and/or executive achievement leadership, and academic standing consistent with appointment at Professor level. Demonstrated academic leadership and achievement in Indigenous education, research, research training or a related area. Experience leading university-wide academic initiatives and influencing outcomes in a complex higher education environment. A strong understanding of academic governance, including boards, committees and university decision-making processes. A track record of developing and delivering initiatives that strengthen Indigenous participation, success and excellence in higher education. Experience building research capability and/or leading curriculum transformation in an Indigenous higher education context. A strong understanding of the national Indigenous higher education, research and policy landscape. Strong connections with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities and cultures. The ability to build respectful, collaborative relationships across faculties, disciplines, communities and stakeholder groups with integrity and collegiality. Experience leading academic and professional staff and developing connected, capable and high-performing teams. Highly developed communication, relationship-building, influencing and advocacy skills. A leadership approach grounded in respect, integrity, cultural safety, care and accountability. A strong commitment to Curtin’s values, strategic priorities and aspirations for Indigenous success. Senior leadership experience within the higher education sector, together with experience working collaboratively with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities to advance education, research and knowledge development, will be highly regarded. If your qualifications or experience look different from what we've described above, but you feel your skills and potential are a match to the capabilities we need, please apply. We are looking for diversity in experience, thought and approach. For more information about this opportunity, including the full position description and selection criteria please review the Candidate Brochure via the following link: https://tinyurl.com/ADVCI-Curtin Role Requirements: You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. Beyond these benefits, this is an opportunity to: Build something meaningful by creating new programs, partnerships and pathways with university-wide impact. Broaden your leadership experience through engagement with faculties, Academic Board, research leaders and senior executives. Connect education and research across Indigenous curriculum, research excellence, HDR development and academic workforce capability. Join a passionate and well-supported team , working closely with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Indigenous and other senior academic leaders. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit, https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! Please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining: your interest in the role how your background aligns with the leadership capabilities, role requirements and selection criteria If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Tuesday 3 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=917919": {
      "title": "Project Coordinator/ Evidence Synthesis and Research Coordinator",
      "description": "Project Coordinator/ Evidence Synthesis and Research Coordinator Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 21/10/2026 Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Full-time, Fixed-term until 30 June 2028 | Bentley Campus Reference: 1684435 About the Role: Curtin University is seeking a motivated Project Coordinator to support impactful evidence synthesis and research translation projects within the Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES) Evidence, Evaluation and Trial (EET) Program. Working closely with the Project Lead, you will coordinate project delivery, undertake systematic literature reviews, screen and analyse research evidence, and contribute to high-quality evidence syntheses and meta-analyses. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in projects that inform practice, strengthen evaluation, and help drive meaningful outcomes in student equity and success. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinate project activities, timelines, deliverables and resources to support successful project outcomes. Work closely with the Project Lead, partners and stakeholders to define objectives, manage priorities and keep projects on track. Develop and maintain project documentation, including work plans, business cases, reports, communication plans and evaluation materials. Monitor budgets, milestones, risks and performance indicators, providing clear updates and recommendations as required. Facilitate stakeholder meetings, focus groups and project events to support collaboration and effective delivery. Conduct comprehensive literature reviews and screen academic papers against inclusion and exclusion criteria. Extract, organise and interpret research data, including effect sizes, study quality and potential risk of bias. Contribute to evidence syntheses, meta-analyses and summaries that inform practice and strengthen student equity outcomes. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): Relevant tertiary qualification with substantial experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Strong project coordination skills, with the ability to manage plans, timelines, resources and deliverables within agreed constraints. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and maintain accuracy and attention to detail. Well-developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders and work sensitively across cultures. Clear and confident written communication skills, including the ability to prepare concise project reports and communicate research findings. Strong research, analytical and statistical skills, including experience identifying, appraising, extracting and interpreting evidence. Experience conducting systematic reviews, evidence synthesis and/or meta-analyses, including screening, quality assessment and data extraction. Sound judgement, problem-solving ability and a collaborative approach, with desirable experience in tertiary education, impact evaluation, project management or providing evidence-related advice and support. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term G07 level role attracts a remuneration of $109,277 - $117,331 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Tuesday, 3 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916916": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Fellow",
      "description": "Research Associate/Fellow Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 17/10/2026 Join an internationally connected radio astronomy research environment Grow your research career and contribute to high-impact astrophysics research Enjoy flexible working arrangements, generous super & salary packaging benefits Job Reference: 1424374 About the Role: As a Research Fellow, you will join Professor James Miller-Jones and the CIRA research group, contributing to research focused on high-angular-resolution radio astronomy and jet physics. The group studies accreting compact objects, including X-ray binaries and tidal disruption events, to better understand how these systems launch outflows in the form of relativistic jets. You will work alongside existing team members to apply and further develop novel techniques for modelling high-angular-resolution radio interferometry data from rapidly evolving sources, including X-ray binary jets. This is an excellent opportunity to develop your research profile within a highly collaborative environment while contributing to research at the forefront of radio astronomy and astrophysics. Your responsibilities will include: Contributing to high-quality research outputs and publications. Participating in applications for competitive research funding. Presenting and disseminating research findings through seminars, workshops and conferences. Contributing to the supervision of undergraduate and postgraduate research students. Undertaking research-related administrative activities. Contributing to academic service, engagement and activities across CIRA and the broader University. Who We Are Looking For - Selection Criteria: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline (or with no more than ~6 months until completion at the time of interview), such as astronomy, astrophysics, physics, statistics or computer science. Demonstrated knowledge in a relevant discipline and experience with appropriate research techniques. Experience undertaking qualitative and quantitative research tasks. An emerging research track record and demonstrated potential to undertake high-quality research and contribute productively within research teams. Strong organisational, communication and interpersonal skills. A demonstrated commitment to supporting a collegial and inclusive working environment and working effectively with people from diverse backgrounds. Familiarity with time-domain astrophysics, particularly jets from X-ray binary systems. Desirable: Experience working with radio interferometry data, particularly very long baseline interferometry. Experience or skills in statistical modelling and Bayesian inference. Familiarity with X-ray binaries, astronomical jets or time-domain astrophysics. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. We will also consider international candidates who are eligible for a relevant visa. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Your new Team - The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA): CIRA is a multidisciplinary research institute bringing together researchers and professional staff from science, engineering and computing to tackle complex challenges in radio astronomy. CIRA is the lead organisation and operator of the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA) and a key participant in the international Square Kilometre Array (SKA) project. CIRA's research spans a diverse range of projects involving and motivated by radio astronomy, providing an exciting and collaborative environment for researchers seeking to contribute to internationally significant astrophysics research. To learn more about the Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy and its research, visit: www.astronomy.curtin.edu.au www.icrar.org www.mwatelescope.org www.skao.int Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we are passionate about fostering an inclusive, diverse and welcoming environment in which all people feel respected, valued and safe. Curtin is dedicated to promoting gender equity and inclusivity in all areas. We are proud of our achievements to date and recognise that there is more to do to improve employment outcomes for women and people of diverse gender identities, particularly in STEMM disciplines. As an Athena SWAN accredited institution, we celebrate diversity and actively work to advance the representation of women and gender-diverse people in STEMM areas and empower individuals so they can thrive in their careers through a range of supportive initiatives, including leadership training, mentorship for early career progression and promotion, networking opportunities, and other professional development resources. As reflected in the Athena Swan Charter, gender equality is a broader issue that extends beyond barriers affecting women alone, and we are committed to addressing these challenges holistically. Additionally, we offer staff a competitive remuneration and benefits package, including but not limited to: 17% superannuation, plus a variety of salary packaging options. Paid Parental leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver and 3 weeks for non-primary) subject to eligibility criteria outlined in the Enterprise Agreement; personal leave (14 days) and annual leave (20 days) Enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year (including Easter Tuesday!), and 2 extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year Period (see EBA 51.1 and 51.9). Breastfeeding facilities and campus-based childcare (where priority of placement is given to Curtin staff and students) Flexible work arrangements including return to work provisions On-campus medical centre Academic Carer Support Scheme and other diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives detailed via the below link. We believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds to join us in shaping the future of STEMM together and making tomorrow better. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/ About Curtin University Curtin proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This three-year fixed term, full-time ALA/ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $104,165 - $144,352 (ALA.5 - ALB.6) + 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a variety food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience A document addressing the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Monday, 16 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920818": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer",
      "description": "Administrative Officer Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 28/10/2026 Full time, permanent position | Curtin Bentley Campus | commencing Jan 2027 Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1697353 About the Role: Join the Curtin School of Allied Health as an Administrative Officer and play a key role in keeping the School’s operations running smoothly. This is a full-time (1.0 FTE), continuing opportunity to join a collaborative team and contribute to a busy and diverse academic environment. The position will support the School’s scheduling and coordination requirements for student examinations, providing end-to-end operational support across exam scheduling, venue and invigilator coordination, and the management of examination materials. As part of the School Operations Team, you will work closely with academic staff and University teams to ensure activities run smoothly, while also supporting day-to-day School operations, maintaining shared information repositories and contributing to continuous improvement initiatives. If you are highly organised, detail-focused and confident managing multiple priorities, we invite you to bring your skills and experience to this varied and rewarding role. This role will be commencing in January 2027. Your responsibilities will include: Establishing and maintaining efficient administrative processes and practices to support the day-to-day operations of the School Providing administrative and logistical support for examinations, projects and other School activities Liaising with School stakeholders and University functional areas to ensure administrative activities are coordinated and completed effectively Administering communications and correspondence, ensuring information is provided accurately, professionally and in a timely manner Maintaining accurate and up-to-date shared information repositories and records Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Qualifications : Completion of a diploma or equivalent qualification with relevant work experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and/or experience Administrative Coordination : Demonstrated experience coordinating administrative activities and processes to support efficient day-to-day operations Technical Proficiency : Strong proficiency in Microsoft Office applications, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook, with the ability to confidently use technology and systems to support administrative activities Organisational Skills : Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and maintain a high level of accuracy and attention to detail Communication Skills : Well-developed written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate professionally and effectively with a diverse range of stakeholders Problem Solving : Demonstrated problem-solving skills, with the ability to exercise sound judgement, use initiative and determine appropriate courses of action Teamwork and Independence : Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively within a team to achieve shared outcomes Desirable Previous experience in a university setting Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new team: School of Allied Health The School of Allied Health at Curtin University is a vibrant, collaborative, and community-focused school dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of people across Western Australia and beyond. Our teaching, research, and partnerships span a wide range of allied health disciplines, including exercise and sport science, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, speech pathology and social work. We are deeply committed to delivering an exceptional student experience - one that prepares graduates to be compassionate, skilled, and practice-ready professionals who make meaningful contributions to the communities they serve. Our staff are passionate educators, researchers, and practitioners who work closely with industry partners, health services, and community organisations to ensure our programs remain relevant, innovative, and responsive to emerging health needs. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time, permanent position, with a salary range between $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on 12 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920845": {
      "title": "FinOps Lead",
      "description": "FinOps Lead Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 28/10/2026 Full time, continuing (permanent) position Generous leave entitlements and 17% superannuation Work-life balance options, inc. potential flexible work-from-home arrangements Job Reference: 1700919 About the Role We are looking for an experienced and commercially minded FinOps leader to play a key role in shaping Curtin University’s cloud financial management capability. This is an exciting opportunity for someone with strong technical insight, sound financial judgement, and the confidence to influence change in a complex digital environment. Working within our Digital Platforms Team , you will lead cloud financial operations and help ensure Curtin gains maximum value from its cloud investments through strong governance, transparency, accountability and continuous improvement. As a trusted leader in Curtin’s cloud journey, you will champion a cost-conscious culture across the university, partnering with technical and business stakeholders to embed value, efficiency and financial responsibility into the way digital platforms and services are designed, delivered and operated. This role brings together cloud expertise, financial acumen, stakeholder engagement and people leadership, offering the chance to make a visible impact as Curtin continues to mature its digital strategy and cloud capability. Key Responsibilities: Lead and mentor a FinOps team to deliver strategic cloud financial management outcomes and uplift FinOps capability across the university. Develop and implement strategies to optimise cloud costs, improve value from cloud investments and support financially sustainable cloud consumption. Provide clear visibility and insight into cloud spend across compute, storage, applications and broader cloud services. Develop and enforce cost allocation models, tagging standards, and chargeback/showback frameworks to improve financial transparency, governance and accountability. Leverage data insights to analyse spend trends, identify optimisation opportunities and provide informed recommendations to executive stakeholders. Collaborate with technology leaders and business stakeholders to promote efficient cloud utilisation, support practical change and strengthen financially responsible practices. Lead education, engagement and continuous improvement initiatives to champion FinOps principles, build cloud financial literacy and mature cloud financial management tools, reporting platforms and cost monitoring capabilities. Selection Criteria: We are seeking an experienced leader with strong financial operations expertise, the ability to influence at a strategic level, and a solid understanding of enterprise cloud environments. You will bring: Demonstrated experience in FinOps, cloud financial management, technology finance, or cloud cost optimisation within a complex enterprise environment. Strong commercial and financial acumen, including budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, and cost optimisation practices. Working knowledge of cloud platforms such as AWS and/or Microsoft Azure, including cloud billing, consumption, and optimisation principles. Experience implementing or managing FinOps practices such as chargeback/showback models, cloud spend reporting, and cost governance frameworks. Excellent stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to influence and educate both technical and non-technical audiences. Proven leadership capability, with experience driving strategic initiatives, leading teams, and building collaborative relationships across an organisation. A proactive and strategic mindset, with the ability to identify opportunities, drive continuous improvement, and shape organisational direction. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new Team: Digital, Technology & Solutions, Platforms The Platforms Team is the engine room of Curtin University’s digital ecosystem. We design, build and operate the core technology platforms that underpin the entire university - from cloud infrastructure and automation to data, integration, and foundational digital services. Our work enables every faculty, researcher, student and staff member to innovate, connect and succeed. This is a unique opportunity to shape and mature FinOps capability within one of Australia’s leading universities. You will have the opportunity to influence enterprise-wide cloud financial strategy, drive meaningful operational efficiencies, build a community of practice, and help embed a culture of financial accountability and cloud optimisation across the university. The role offers a rare combination of strategic influence, leadership responsibility, and the opportunity to make a visible impact within a large and evolving digital environment. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous G10 level role attracts a remuneration of $152,905 - $167,669 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume A document addressing the selection criteria for the role If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Wednesday, 11 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies, at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920732": {
      "title": "Safer Community Officers - Kalgoorlie",
      "description": "Safer Community Officers - Kalgoorlie Office of Corporate Services Kalgoorlie, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 28/10/2026 Competitive package + 17% super and regional allowances Help shape Curtin's new in-house Safer Community service Permanent full-time role with rent support and generous leave Job Reference: 1692770 An Exciting Opportunity for 2027! With our new in-house Safer Community service set to launch in early 2027, now is the perfect time to explore your next career opportunity with Curtin University. We're recruiting ahead of this exciting transition, giving successful candidates the opportunity to join a newly established team and help shape the future of our Safer Community service at the Kalgoorlie Campus. About the Role We are seeking Safer Community Officers to join Curtin University as we establish our in-house Safer Community service at the Kalgoorlie Campus. As a Safer Community Officer, you will provide a visible, approachable and reassuring presence across campus, supporting the safety, security and wellbeing of students, staff and visitors. While security is an important part of the role, Curtin’s Safer Community approach goes further. You will combine proactive patrols and incident response with strong customer service, early intervention and wellbeing support. No two shifts will be quite the same. You may be responding to a safety or security incident, supporting someone experiencing distress, providing a safety escort, assisting with events, liaising with emergency services or simply being a trusted and visible point of contact for members of the campus community. Key Responsibilities: Patrol campus on foot, bicycle and vehicle. Provide safety escorts and an approachable point of contact. Respond to incidents and assist emergency services. Triage wellbeing concerns and connect people with support. Record incidents and assist with events and traffic management. Skills & Experience: Certificate II in Security Operations from a recognised provider. Current Western Australian driver’s licence and Senior First Aid certificate. Strong communication and customer-service skills. Responds sensitively to people from diverse backgrounds. Available for an operational shift roster and fit for patrol duties. Mental Health First Aid training is desirable. Role Requirements Ability to work outside normal office hours and participate in an operational shift roster. Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Properties Facilities & Development Curtin’s Properties Facilities & Development team supports the safe, effective and reliable operation of the University’s physical environment. In Kalgoorlie, the team provides facilities and maintenance support across the campus, student accommodation and Curtin’s expanding residential housing portfolio. Working across everything from laboratories and lecture theatres to accommodation and residential properties, you will be exposed to a variety of facilities and specialised environments, while working closely with university staff, contractors and service providers to keep our facilities safe, functional and well maintained. Curtin Kalgoorlie is located in the heart of Western Australia’s historic Goldfields. Home to the renowned WA School of Mines, the campus has a strong community atmosphere and provides the opportunity to live and work in one of Australia’s most important resources regions. What We Offer You Competitive remuneration: $69,000–$75,046 (G03) plus 17% superannuation, applicable locality and shift allowances. Locality allowance: $2,354 per annum, with increased entitlements for eligible employees with dependants. Additional earning opportunities: Potential overtime payments, subject to operational requirements. Generous leave entitlements: 20 days' annual leave, increasing to 25 days for eligible shift workers, plus 14 days' personal leave and 17.5% annual leave loading. Regional benefits: Two additional days of annual leave when travelling to Perth during annual leave, subject to eligibility. Family-friendly benefits: Up to 26 weeks paid parental leave for eligible primary caregivers. Additional leave: Five days' Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander leave for eligible employees, plus study leave and community service leave provisions. Extra time off: University-observed public holidays, including Easter Tuesday, and two additional University Days over Christmas/New Year, subject to roster arrangements. Employee discounts: Salary packaging through Paywise and access to selected hotel and health insurance discounts. Long-service leave: Potential recognition of eligible prior service. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join us! Please click APPLY NOW , ensuring you attach the following documents: Cover letter outlining your skills, knowledge and experience relevant to the position. Resume. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our Careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 9.00pm AWST, 12 November 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921418": {
      "title": "Business Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Business Operations Coordinator Office of Corporate Services Kalgoorlie, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 29/10/2026 Full time, permanent role l Kalgoorlie l Commencement January 2027 Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Attractive benefits including generous leave entitlements! Reference : 1689574 About the Role: This is an opportunity to take a hands-on role at the heart of Curtin’s residential community in Kalgoorlie, coordinating the day-to-day business operations that help Agricola run effectively while contributing to a positive experience for residents. As Business Operations Coordinator, you will bring together operational coordination, administration, stakeholder engagement and resident support. Working closely with the Accommodation Manager, accommodation staff, University service areas and external stakeholders, you will help ensure operational matters are managed efficiently while supporting a welcoming and well-connected residential community. The role offers genuine variety. Alongside coordinating business processes, reporting, resources and administration, you will contribute to residential life programs, pastoral care and activities that encourage community living, cross-cultural connection, health, wellbeing and participation. You will also support the Accommodation Manager when complex or critical issues arise. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinate the effective day-to-day business operations of Agricola in partnership with University service areas and external stakeholders. Develop and maintain efficient administrative processes, communications, reports and correspondence. Provide clear advice on relevant policies, processes and operational matters. Coordinate residential life, social interaction and pastoral care initiatives that support community living, cross-cultural connection, health, hygiene and sporting activities. Support resource planning, budget monitoring, staffing administration and the supervision of administrative staff where required. Act as a key contact for residents and a broad range of internal and external stakeholders. Support the Accommodation Manager in responding to critical incidents, emergencies, and behavioural or disciplinary matters in line with relevant processes. Who we are looking for/Selection Criteria: You will be an organised and approachable professional who enjoys bringing people and processes together. You will be comfortable managing competing priorities, working independently when required and building trusted relationships with people from a wide variety of backgrounds. Relevant tertiary qualifications and relevant experience in residential accommodation or property management; extensive experience and broad knowledge in an administrative field; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education or training. Demonstrated experience coordinating, or the ability to coordinate, the operational requirements of a small business unit. Highly developed organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple deliverables in a dynamic environment. Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to build rapport and work effectively with a diverse range of internal and external stakeholders. Highly effective written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to present information clearly and professionally. The ability to work collaboratively as part of a team and independently with limited direction. Sound judgement and the ability to resolve day-to-day and critical issues appropriately. An understanding of issues relevant to young people and the capacity to provide pastoral care within a residential environment. Desirable : Previous experience in tertiary education, particularly student accommodation or working with international students, and experience mentoring young people. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Ability to work outside normal office hours when required, including participation in an on-call roster and responding to calls from residential staff on weeknights and weekends. Current Class C driver’s license. Desirable Mental Health and or First Aid Certificate. (successful applicant to complete this training once they commence) Your new Team: Accommodation, Operating Engagement Services Based at Agricola in Kalgoorlie, you will work closely with the Accommodation Manager and accommodation team, including Residential Assistants and Senior Residential Assistants. The role also works across Curtin with Student Experience services, WASM, Properties, People and Culture, Financial Services, Digital & Technology Solutions and the Curtin Safer Community Team. Your stakeholder network will extend beyond the University to current and prospective residents, families and guardians, suppliers, contractors, external organisations and emergency services where required. It is a varied role where strong relationships, sound judgement and responsive coordination make a tangible contribution to the residential community. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous G06 level role attracts a remuneration of $99,213 - $105,251 per annum + 17% superannuation . Time Well-Spent: Experience the added value of 17.5% leave loading, with the option to be paid out or taken as additional leave. Benefit from Parental Leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver), Annual Leave (25 days), Personal Leave (14 days), Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Leave (5 days) * Travel Leave (2 days), Study Leave and Community Service Leave. Plus 18% more observed days off – enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year, including Easter Tuesday, and two extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year period. Recognition for Prior Service: Curtin recognises prior service for long service leave purposes. New Curtin employees may be* eligible if they have worked for an Australian university, public service or other recognised institution. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages , OR A document addressing the selection criteria for the role If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday 22nd of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921993": {
      "title": "Facility Veterinary Officer",
      "description": "Facility Veterinary Officer Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Part Time 31/10/2026 Part time (0.6 FTE), permanent | Curtin Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1693601 About the Role: Curtin University is seeking an experienced veterinary professional to join us as a Facility Veterinary Officer on a part-time (0.6 FTE), permanent basis. Reporting to the Technical Operations Manager, you will play an important role in ensuring animals used in research and teaching are cared for to the highest standards of veterinary care and regulatory compliance. Working across Curtin’s animal facilities, you will provide veterinary leadership and strategic advice on the use, and care of a diverse range of animals, from laboratory and aquatic species through to avian and native wildlife. The role offers considerable variety and the opportunity to use your veterinary expertise across a broad research and teaching environment. You will oversee Curtin’s veterinary care program, providing specialist advice on animal health, husbandry, welfare and biosecurity while helping to shape and promote best-practice animal care across the University. Importantly, you will work alongside researchers undertaking medical and biomedical research, providing veterinary expertise that supports their work to better understand disease and advance medical research. This is an opportunity to have a genuine influence on the ethical use and care of animals across Curtin, combining animal welfare, veterinary leadership and meaningful research impact in a unique university environment. Your responsibilities will include: Provide veterinary leadership and strategic advice on the health, care and welfare of animals used for research and teaching Oversee Curtin’s veterinary care program, ensuring compliance with the NHMRC Australian Code for the Care and Use of Animals for Scientific Purposes Lead the diagnosis and treatment of animals within Curtin’s animal facilities, including prescribing treatments and supervising restricted drug use Develop and deliver training in animal handling, procedures and care, and assess the competency of staff and researchers working with animals Provide specialist veterinary advice to Animal Facility Managers and the Technical Operations Manager on animal health, care and welfare Provide pre-AEC application consultation and veterinary advice to researchers and other animal users Who we are looking for (selection criteria): A tertiary qualification in Veterinary Science or extensive veterinary experience Eligibility for veterinary registration in Western Australia Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to effectively engage with animal stakeholders and University management In-depth knowledge of legislation, standards and codes of practice governing the care and use of animals in research and teaching environments Demonstrated ability to work independently, provide specialist advice and lead and influence within your area of expertise A strong commitment to the welfare and ethical care of animals used for research and teaching Demonstrated proficiency in the responsible care and management of animals Desirable criteria: Experience working within an animal facility in a tertiary education or research environment Knowledge and/or experience in laboratory animal surgical techniques Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered 22.5 hours per week. Successful candidate will have the opportunity to negotiate their preferred working schedule Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Availability to respond to work-related matters outside standard business hours, where required Flexibility to attend animal facilities outside standard business hours, including weekends, where required About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This part time (0.6 FTE), permanent role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 (G08) pro rata to 0.6 FTE + 17% superannuation Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921989": {
      "title": "Business Development Manager - Translation and Impact",
      "description": "Business Development Manager - Translation and Impact Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 31/10/2026 Fixed term role until 30 June 2028. Part-time or full-time (minimum 0.7 FTE); Translate specialist STEM capability into meaningful real-word opportunities Competitive remuneration, leave, super & salary packaging provisions Reference Number: 1701413 About the Team: Radio Astronomy applies ‘STEM’ based disciplines to capture and analyse ultra-faint signals from the universe. In doing this, we push the boundaries of technology and techniques in electromagnetics, signal processing, software development, and radio frequency communications. The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) and The International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) deliver a dedicated Translation and Impact program (T&I) with a strong reputation for forging meaningful, mutually beneficial collaborations with industry to share our skills, knowledge, and expertise for mutual benefit. By collaborating with experts in other domains, we can help boost capability, train WA’s future workforce, grow businesses, and create jobs. About the Role: You will work with the Director, Translation and Impact to identify, develop, manage, and provide broad support to the technical experts who deliver on collaborative projects. In this role you will develop a thorough appreciation of the capabilities and underlying skill sets of our team (including physics, electrical engineering, and data science), and matching them to opportunities for collaboration and impact outside of radio astronomy. You will engage with a variety of internal and external stakeholders, including State, Commonwealth, and international governments, various industries (commonly, Defence and Space), and other research organisations. This will involve identifying and leveraging funding opportunities to support ICRAR’s Translation and Impact objectives. You will be responsible for providing broad project support, including operational support, and navigating internal Curtin policies and procedures to enable our subject matter experts to deliver on commercial projects as required. This will involve budgeting, commercial contract negotiation, contract delivery, and reporting. Please note will consider applicants seeking a full time or part time position (minimum 0.7FTE), with some flexibility as to working hours (having regard to individual project or business requirements). Your responsibilities will include: Building relationships with, liaising with, and maintaining communication and productive relationships with relevant partners and stakeholders. Managing all activities related to assigned projects, from start up, planning, implementation and close-out, to achieve the defined project outcomes. This will include liaising with internal University service functions (e.g. Research Office, Risk & Compliance, International Risk and Security, People and Culture, Legal Services, Financial Services). Developing relevant business cases, funding proposals, and project initiation documentation, including work plans, timelines, costings, budgets, commercial contracts, and communication strategies. Identifying, source, manage and assign necessary project resources. Identifying impacts and risks associated with the project and reporting on and managing these. This may include field trip risk assessments, safety protocols and logistics. Preparing documents required for the export of certain technical goods and services, including Defence Export Controls and other customs requirements, ensuring compliance with Curtin University policies and procedures. Managing internal and external resources associated with the delivery of assigned projects, and managing the procurement of any third party services, ensuring relevant practices and processes are adhered to. Who We Are Looking For (Selection Criteria): We are looking for someone personable, with experience applying commercial and political acumen, servicing a combination of stakeholders across industry, government, and not for profit sectors. The scope of your role can vary significantly between individual projects, stakeholders, and activities. For that reason, you will be comfortable working in an environment that is more driven by specific project needs than by explicit rote tasks. In particular, you will be comfortable adapting rapidly to a diversity of tasks/roles, to prioritise workloads, and multitask, as opportunities and initiatives move through their lifecycle. You will be comfortable working alongside technical subject matter experts to understand and communicate effectively our industry-relevant capabilities. Previous experience in a relevant technical discipline (e.g. involving physical sciences, computing, or engineering) will enable you to understand and credibly communicate CIRA capabilities to stakeholders. Essential Criteria: Relevant tertiary qualifications and extensive experience in business development and industry engagement within a complex organisation; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education training. Demonstrated commercial acumen and a successful track record in securing and integrating funding support from industry, government and other organisations into collaborative ventures. Demonstrated experience in contract negotiation and management. Demonstrated ability to engage and influence others and a proven capacity to build and maintain effective and productive relationships with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders. Demonstrated high level communication (written and oral) with the ability to engage and present information clearly and informatively to diverse audiences and prepare business cases. Demonstrated experience in conducting analytical research and providing reports on public documents to identify significant information. Desirable: Familiarity with commercial contract terms and conditions generally, and ideally (but not necessarily) the negotiation of commercial contract terms or contract management in any sector will be highly valued. Ideally (but not necessarily) some experience supporting demand-driven (commercial) research or working in a university environment. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction . Your new Team – CIRA: The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) is a multi-disciplinary research institute engaged in a range of projects involving or motivated by radio astronomy. CIRA’s activities draw together academic research and professional staff from a variety of science, engineering, and computing disciplines to tackle unique and demanding challenges in astronomy and non-astronomy domains. Committed to research translation and impact (T&I), CIRA is committed to engaging with partners to share our skills, knowledge, and expertise for mutual benefit and impact beyond astronomy. CIRA is the lead organisation and operator of the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA), a key participant in the international SKA Project. CIRA is also the Curtin University node of the International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) which was established in 2009, and one of the largest and most enduring academic research initiatives in Western Australia. CIRA celebrates diversity and is an equal opportunity employer. We encourage applications from all cultures, races, nationalities, genders, identities, and abilities aligning with the selection criteria, and are committed to preserving our principles of equitable and inclusive work and study environments. To learn more about CIRA and our projects and initiatives, please visit the below websites: www.astronomy.curtin.edu.au www.icrar.org About Curtin University We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This fixed term, full-time (or part-time at 0.7 FTE or higher), G08 level role attracts a full-time remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 (pro rata for part-time) + 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest, experience and addressing the section criteria for the role (no more than two pages) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8pm AWST, Sunday, 15 November 2026 Disclaimer: Please note that there is no standardised time for the closing of adverts on the specified date. Therefore, we recommend submitting your application ahead of this deadline. Late applications will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921888": {
      "title": "Systems Support Officer (s51)",
      "description": "Systems Support Officer (s51) Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 31/10/2026 Full-time opportunity until 31 December 2027 | Bentley Campus Support the OASIS Enhancement Program and help make everyday digital experiences Flexible working arrangements | 2 days per week from home Job Reference: 1684428 At Curtin we are committed to embedding the voices and perspectives of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples at the forefront of our decision-making to create an environment in which everyone thrives. In line with Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Plan, Curtin University is committed to optimising employment outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. In an effort to increase Aboriginal and Torres Strait representation in the University, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are strongly encouraged to apply for this role, pursuant to Section 51 of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA). About the Role At Curtin University, we're transforming how students connect with the information, services and support they need throughout their university journey. Our OASIS Enhancement Program is an exciting initiative designed to create a more connected, intuitive and personalised digital experience for students. Building on the successful refresh of our student portal, the program will introduce new functionality to support student orientation, academic planning, communications and campus engagement. We're looking for a motivated and solutions-focused Systems Support Officer to join our Development and User Experience team and contribute to this important program of work. Key responsibilities include: Manage incoming enquiries and support tickets, keeping users informed and escalating complex matters. Support the Senior Business Analyst with workflow coordination and day-to-day systems administration. Assist with OASIS access, provide user guidance and training, and maintain clear process documentation. Work with developers, UX analysts and colleagues to resolve issues, capture feedback and support ongoing improvements. SELECTION CRITERIA We welcome applicants with transferable experience in administration, student services, service desks or systems support. To be successful, you will demonstrate: A relevant degree or equivalent combination of experience, education and training. Experience supporting users, administering processes or working with digital systems, with a willingness to learn. Strong organisation, problem-solving and attention to detail, including managing competing requests and knowing when to escalate. Clear, culturally respectful communication and a collaborative approach to supporting people with varied technical confidence. Experience with ticketing systems such as ServiceNow, access administration or user training is desirable, not essential. Role Requirements As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, fixed-term role attracts a salary package of $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) per annum plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience, addressing the Selection Criteria noted above If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00PM (AWST), 13 November 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921847": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Co-curricular Development",
      "description": "Coordinator, Co-curricular Development Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 31/10/2026 Full-time, continuing (permanent) opportunity | Curtin Perth (Bentley) Campus Commencing January 2027 | Help students build skills beyond the classroom Grow Curtin Extra and strengthen student engagement and employability outcomes Job Reference: 1701292 About the Role Curtin University is seeking a collaborative and creative Coordinator, Co-curricular Development to champion high-quality co-curricular participation and development across the University. You’ll work with students, staff and partners to strengthen the quality, reach and impact of co-curricular opportunities, with a particular focus on improving equitable participation for students from low socioeconomic status (LSES) backgrounds and First Nations students. Through this work, you’ll help students make the most of experiences that enrich their studies and strengthen their graduate outcomes. A key focus of the role is Curtin Extra, our framework for enhancing students’ co-curricular learning and recognising their participation and achievements. You’ll help students understand and articulate the skills they develop through these experiences, while supporting quality programs and effective governance across Curtin. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinate the growth, delivery, governance and evaluation of the Curtin Extra framework and support high-quality co-curricular programs across Curtin. Help students recognise and articulate the skills and capabilities they develop through co-curricular participation. Partner with university teams, students and external organisations to expand equitable access to high-quality co-curricular opportunities, particularly for underrepresented student groups. Build awareness of Curtin Extra through advocacy, presentations, training, resources and promotional initiatives. Support effective governance, data systems, participation records, compliance, evaluation and recognition processes. Use student engagement data and program evaluation to identify opportunities for improvement and strengthen student and graduate outcomes. SELECTION CRITERIA You are a relationship-focused program coordinator who enjoys bringing people together, developing new ideas and translating them into meaningful student experiences. You balance creativity with strong organisation and sound governance. To be successful, you will demonstrate: A relevant qualification with considerable relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training. Demonstrated experience managing complex projects and delivering effective programs or services. Ability to develop and implement processes and strategies that improve quality and outcomes. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to influence and build cooperation across diverse stakeholder groups. Excellent organisational skills, with experience coordinating people and events, prioritising work and using initiative. Creative problem-solving skills and the ability to contribute effectively within a busy, collaborative and culturally diverse team. Confidence using multiple systems, CRM platforms and reporting tools. Competence in using databases, student management systems and digital platforms, together with an understanding of student engagement, equity and inclusion in higher education, would be highly regarded. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, applicants require permanent working rights in Australia to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a criminal record check and relevant background, integrity and reference checks in accordance with University requirements. The role may require work outside normal office hours. Your new Team: – Student Life and Community Based within Student Life and Community, you’ll contribute to Curtin’s student success and community development priorities. The role works closely with students, academic and professional colleagues, the Curtin Student Guild and industry and community partners to support co-curricular learning and recognition across Curtin’s campuses and partner institutions. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, continuing (permanent) role attracts a salary package of $109,277 - $117,331 (G07) per annum plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8.00PM (AWST), 13 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921691": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching Focused) - Digital & Social Media",
      "description": "Lecturer (Teaching Focused) - Digital & Social Media Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 31/10/2026 Fixed-term, Part time 0.5FTE contract Ending 14th of January 2028 Based on Bentley Campus WA Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation, collaboration, & sust Reference: 1700894 About the Role: Support student learning as a teaching-focused Lecturer in digital and social media. In this part-time role (0.5 FTE), you will deliver engaging teaching, teaching both face-to-face and online, develop innovative learning materials and contribute to curriculum design and unit coordination. Your primary contribution will be to teaching and learning, drawing on current research, scholarship and professional practice to enrich the student experience. You will maintain your discipline knowledge, develop your teaching practice and scholarly activities, and build your profile in teaching, academic service and leadership within a collegial and supportive environment. Your responsibilities will include: Teaching and learning: Plan and deliver engaging online and face-to-face learning activities in digital and social media, drawing on current discipline knowledge and research-informed teaching practices. Curriculum and coordination: Collaborate with colleagues to design innovative curricula and learning resources, coordinate units, and undertake associated teaching administration. Assessment and feedback: Assess and moderate student work, provide constructive feedback and tailor learning support to help students achieve the intended learning outcomes. Student support and supervision: Support students’ transition to university and respond to diverse learning needs. Potentially supervise honours and postgraduate coursework students, including master’s-level research projects, and higher degree by research students as appropriate. Teaching scholarship and development: Maintain current discipline knowledge, incorporate research, scholarship and professional practice into teaching, and contribute to professional development and peer review to continually improve student learning. Service and engagement: Contribute to academic service, discipline and professional activities, and community engagement, while fostering a supportive working environment and modelling professional conduct consistent with the University’s Code of Conduct and values. Who We Are Looking For/Selection Criteria: We are seeking an educator with expertise in digital and social media, a commitment to high-quality, inclusive teaching and a collaborative approach to academic life. To be successful in this role, you will have: A doctoral qualification in digital and social media or a relevant discipline. Demonstrated high-level teaching skills and a reflective approach to teaching delivery, including online teaching delivery. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline. An emerging record of contribution to the scholarship of teaching. Demonstrated experience in, or capacity to undertake, unit coordination. High-level communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, a commitment to a collegial and supportive working environment, and the ability to engage with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. A demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in day-to-day work. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role This is a part-time role (0.5 FTE). Working days will be discussed at interview. Please indicate your preferred working days in your cover letter, including whether these days are flexible. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Part-Time 0.5 Fixed Term ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $121,857 - $144,352 pro rata to 0.5 FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages, OR A document addressing the selection criteria for the role If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Thursday 12th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921587": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Associate Lecturer - Korean Studies",
      "description": "Lecturer / Associate Lecturer - Korean Studies Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 31/10/2026 Fixed-term Full-time 12 months from commencement date, Bentley Campus Competitive Remuneration package, 17% super & generous leave Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide Reference: 1700086 About the Role: Curtin University is seeking an Associate Lecturer (ALA) or Lecturer (ALB) in Korean Studies, specialising in Korean language, for a teaching and research position. You will contribute to the delivery, coordination and further development of the Korean Studies program, with a particular focus on online teaching, while undertaking research relevant to the your disciplinary expertise in connection to the Korea Research & Engagement Centre at Curtin University. In this role, you will play a central part in strengthening and expanding Australia’s first fully online Korean Studies major, delivered by Curtin and offered nationally through the Open Universities Australia (OUA) network. This teaching and research position also contributes to academic service and engagement. Responsibilities will reflect the appointment level: at ALA, you will develop your teaching and research profile with guidance from colleagues; at ALB, you will take on unit coordination and contribute an established research record, funding experience and research supervision capability. The balance of teaching and research will be agreed through Curtin’s Career and Performance Planning (PCP) process. Your responsibilities will include: Deliver engaging Korean language teaching from beginner to advanced levels, particularly online, developing students’ speaking, listening, reading and writing skills, assessing their work and supporting their learning. Develop and refine curriculum, learning materials and assessment to strengthen the online Korean Studies major. Contribute to unit coordination and teaching administration, with guidance appropriate to your experience and appointment level. Undertake research relevant to Korean Studies, publish scholarly outputs and contribute to the area’s major Academy of Korean Studies Core Research project and [JE1]support with further competitive funding applications. Contribute to honours and postgraduate supervision, as appropriate to your qualifications and appointment level. Participate in academic service, community engagement and a collaborative, supportive working environment. At ALA, you will develop your teaching and research profile with guidance from colleagues. At ALB, you will take greater responsibility for unit coordination, research funding applications, supervision and academic service. Who We Are Looking For: We are seeking an academic who can contribute to Korean language education, particularly online, and research relevant to Korean Studies. Applicants do not need to have their PhD in language education, but they should address the shared criteria below and the requirements for their nominated appointment level. High-level Korean language proficiency in speaking, listening, reading and writing, with demonstrated ability to teach Korean from beginner to advanced levels and a reflective approach to improving teaching and supporting student learning. Knowledge of contemporary language teaching approaches and the ability to contribute to engaging online learning materials and curriculum development. High-level communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively and interact with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in teaching, research and day-to-day university activities. Associate Lecturer (ALA) Selection Criteria: Progress towards a doctoral qualification in Korean Studies or a relevant discipline. An understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to Korean language teaching. An emerging research track record or demonstrated potential to conduct quality research relevant to Korean Studies and contribute productively to the Korea Research & Engagement Centre project. Demonstrated ability to contribute to curriculum review and renewal, including Korean language learning materials. Demonstrated high-level organisational skills to manage teaching, research and associated administrative responsibilities. Lecturer (ALB) Selection Criteria: A doctoral qualification in Korean Studies or a relevant discipline. Demonstrated high-level teaching skills relevant to Korean language education and a reflective approach to teaching delivery. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline. An established or emerging record of research outputs relevant to Korean Studies in high-quality refereed academic journals or other appropriate scholarly outlets, relative to opportunity. A history of successful competitive grant applications. Demonstrated ability to supervise higher degree research students. Demonstrated experience or capacity to undertake unit coordination, relevant to the delivery and development of Korean language units. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term role attracts a remuneration of $85,872 - $115,862 (ALA) / $121,857 - $144,352 (ALB) + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover letter outlining your interest in the role and clearly stating whether you are applying for Associate Lecturer (ALA) or Lecturer (ALB) . Demonstrate how your background meets the shared selection criteria and those specific to your nominated level, as outlined above. Please keep your letter to 2–3 pages (maximum 3 pages). If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Thursday 12th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=922003": {
      "title": "Faculty Business Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Faculty Business Operations Coordinator Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 31/10/2026 Full-time, permanent position | Curtin Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration, leave, super & salary packaging provisions On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities. Reference Number: 1700881 About the Role: We are seeking an experienced and proactive individual to join the Faculty of Science and Engineering as Faculty Business Operations Coordinator. You will coordinate operational and administrative functions for the Faculty Office, support a small team and work closely with internal service providers to deliver efficient and responsive services. This is an opportunity to make your mark, with scope to review existing ways of working, simplify processes and develop efficient, well-documented systems that support consistent service delivery. You will have the opportunity to identify improvements, take initiative and help shape how the Faculty operates. The successful candidate will bring strong planning, problem-solving and organisational skills, a process-driven approach and the ability to build effective relationships and motivate a small team to achieve positive outcomes. If you enjoy a dynamic environment and are motivated by improving processes and making a tangible difference, we encourage you to apply. Your responsibilities will include: Manage day-to-day business operations across assigned Faculty areas. Build effective relationships with university service functions and Faculty stakeholders. Develop and improve business processes to support efficient service delivery. Coordinate projects, initiatives and Faculty-wide communications. Provide executive support, including reports, briefing papers and committee support. Manage operational budgets, reporting and resource allocation. Lead and manage the administrative pool, including staffing and performance. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Advanced interpersonal and oral communication skills with the ability to effectively consult with a wide range of stakeholders on a range of matters and in a professional and confident manner. Strong organisational skills with the ability to manage multiple deliverables in a dynamic and complex environment. Developed analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to offer solutions to complex problems. Experience managing staff, with the ability to conduct effective work planning and performance review discussions and to foster a work environment of high achievement, continuous improvement, and job satisfaction. Demonstrated understanding of financial and budget management. High level written communication skills with the ability to prepare high-quality reports, briefing papers and correspondence. Desirable : Previous experience working within the higher education sector. Role Requirements: As this is a permanent position, you will require ongoing work rights in Australia. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new Team: The Faculty of Science and Engineering is committed to delivering transformative education and world-class research across a diverse range of disciplines. With strong industry partnerships and a global presence, the Faculty provides students with innovative, practice-based learning opportunities that prepare them for success in rapidly evolving fields. Our mission is to nurture talent, drive discovery, and contribute to solving real-world challenges through excellence in teaching, research, and engagement. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This continuing (permanent), full-time G07 level role attracts a remuneration of $109,277 - $117,331, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST), Wednesday, 11 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    }
  },
